(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-inner-ear-hearing-loss-vignettes"]={"title":"Inner Ear and Hearing Loss Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 3","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 9-year-old girl with fullness after a cold has a tympanogram whose peak is shifted well into negative pressure. What does this indicate?","choices":["Middle ear fluid","A normal middle ear","Ossicular fixation","A perforated drum","Eustachian tube dysfunction"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a type C trace shows negative middle ear pressure. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo. Syphilis is suspected. Which test should NOT be relied on?","choices":["Microhaemagglutination assay","Audiometry","Otoscopy","The Venereal Disease Research Laboratory test","Fluorescent treponemal antibody testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is specifically described as not helpful here. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman's audiogram shows thresholds of fifty decibels bilaterally. How is her loss classified?","choices":["Mild","Severe","Profound","Normal","Moderate"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the forty to sixty band. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has sudden vertigo and reduced hearing on one side, lasting four days. She is afebrile. What treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Urgent surgery","A hearing aid immediately","Symptomatic treatment with meclizine","Immediate antibiotics","The Epley manoeuvre"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antibiotics are added only if bacterial features appear. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman has hearing loss in the left ear and a whooshing noise in time with her heartbeat. A reddish mass is visible behind the left drum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Middle ear effusion","Acoustic neuroma","Otosclerosis","Glomus tumour"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — a vascular middle ear mass with pulsatile tinnitus. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with tinnitus asks what she can change herself. Which advice fits?","choices":["Sleep as little as possible","Avoid loud noise and stimulants, and rest adequately","Spend an hour daily in complete silence","Increase caffeine intake","Stop all exercise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with daily exercise and having lead levels checked. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 4-year-old girl has bilateral sensorineural hearing loss, a white forelock and eyes of different colours. Which condition does this suggest?","choices":["Waardenburg syndrome","Alport syndrome","Usher syndrome","Meniere disease","Presbycusis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one of the named hereditary causes. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has one-sided hearing loss and understands words far worse than her tone thresholds would predict, with some unsteadiness. Which study should be ordered?","choices":["Computed tomography without contrast","Tympanometry","Plain radiography","The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre","Magnetic resonance imaging with gadolinium"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the gold standard for retrocochlear disease. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man's audiogram is normal at low frequencies and drops steeply above two thousand hertz in both ears. What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Meniere disease","Otosclerosis","A middle ear effusion","Age-related sensorineural loss","A conductive loss from wax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a sloping high-frequency loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has bilateral gradual hearing loss and says background noise makes it harder to follow conversation. Both drums and canals look normal. What Rinne result is expected?","choices":["Bone conduction greater than air, bilaterally","Bone conduction greater than air on the right only","No response to either bilaterally","Bone conduction greater than air on the left only","Air conduction greater than bone, bilaterally"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A sensorineural loss leaves the normal Rinne relationship intact. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman has episodic vertigo thought to arise from one ear. Which study is the gold standard for a disorder affecting one ear at a time?","choices":["The Weber test","Electronystagmography","Pure tone audiometry","Tympanometry","Otoscopy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman has confirmed syphilitic sensorineural hearing loss. What treatment is given?","choices":["An antifungal","Observation","An antibiotic with systemic corticosteroids","Corticosteroids alone","A hearing aid alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 31-year-old surfer has gradual hearing loss and repeated trapping of debris in both canals. Both canals are narrowed symmetrically by hard, smooth swellings of the canal wall. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cerumen impaction","Otitis externa","Canal carcinoma","Cholesteatoma","Exostoses"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — bony growths from repeated cold water exposure. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with unexplained sensorineural hearing loss has multiple risk factors for sexually transmitted infection. What should be considered?","choices":["Human immunodeficiency virus and syphilis","Cerumen impaction","Otosclerosis","Noise-induced loss","Barotrauma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both can produce sensorineural loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has months of unsteadiness and lightheadedness with no ear symptoms. What is the commonest non-vestibular explanation at his age?","choices":["Meniere disease","Labyrinthitis","Wax impaction","Small vessel ischaemic disease","Positional vertigo"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a known acoustic neuroma develops facial numbness and weakness. Which cranial nerves has the tumour involved?","choices":["The fifth and seventh","The third and fourth","The ninth and tenth","The first and second","The eleventh and twelfth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man claims profound hearing loss in both ears following a workplace dispute, yet speaks at a normal volume with normal articulation. What should be suspected?","choices":["Ototoxicity","Otosclerosis","A functional hearing loss","Profound sensorineural loss","Bilateral wax impaction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the normal voice does not fit the claimed loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has reduced hearing on the left. A tuning fork on the forehead is loudest on the left, and on the left bone conduction exceeds air conduction. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":["Meniere disease","Cerumen impaction","Presbycusis","Acoustic neuroma","Ototoxicity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These findings are conductive, and wax is a conductive cause. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has reduced hearing on the right. A tuning fork on the forehead is loudest on the left, and on the right air conduction exceeds bone conduction. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Cerumen impaction","Otosclerosis","Middle ear effusion","Tympanic membrane perforation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These findings are sensorineural, and this is a sensorineural cause. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman developed hearing loss in one ear ten days ago. Which treatment may still help?","choices":["Antibiotic ear drops","Wax removal","Observation for six months","Corticosteroids","A hearing aid alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — acute sensory loss may respond within the first weeks. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has isolated sensorineural hearing loss with no systemic symptoms. Should an autoimmune screen be sent?","choices":["Never, under any circumstances","No — test only when the picture suggests it","Yes, in every patient","Yes, but only if over sixty","Yes, but only in women"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; routine screening is not warranted. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man wakes with profound hearing loss in the right ear. The canal and drum are normal and there is no wax. What is the appropriate action?","choices":["Arrange routine audiometry in three months","Refer to a specialist today","Review in six weeks","Prescribe antibiotic drops","Reassure and take no action"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the corticosteroid window is short. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man on a known ototoxic agent is being reviewed. Which additional organ should be assessed?","choices":["The adrenal gland","The kidney","The liver","The thyroid","The pancreas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ototoxic drugs are frequently nephrotoxic and vice versa. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 22-year-old sound engineer whose hearing recovers by the next day after every shift asks whether that means he is safe. What should he be told?","choices":["Hearing protection is unnecessary below one hundred decibels","The effect is purely psychological","Repeated exposure makes the loss permanent","Full recovery means no damage is accumulating","Only exposures causing pain matter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the temporary shift is a warning, not reassurance. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has fluctuating hearing loss, tinnitus, aural fullness and episodic vertigo. Which treatable cause must be excluded because it is indistinguishable?","choices":["Cerumen impaction","Presbycusis","Exostosis","Syphilis","Otosclerosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — its presentation is often identical, and it is treatable. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man speaks noticeably loudly and struggles most in a crowded room. What does this suggest?","choices":["A sensorineural loss","A conductive loss","Normal hearing","A functional loss","Wax impaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the loud voice and worsening in noise both fit. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"The daughter of an 80-year-old woman with age-related hearing loss asks how to make conversation easier. What advice is best?","choices":["Communicate only in writing","Speak only into her better ear from behind","Face her and speak clearly without shouting","Shout from across the room","Speak quickly to hold her attention"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the problem is discrimination, not volume. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a positive Dix-Hallpike test on the right. What treatment follows?","choices":["Bed rest for two weeks","A hearing aid","The Epley manoeuvre","A course of oral antibiotics","Intratympanic gentamicin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it repositions the debris in the canal. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man is told his hearing loss is conductive. What should he be told about the outlook?","choices":["It is often correctable","It is never correctable","It always progresses to deafness","It will resolve without any treatment","It always requires a cochlear implant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the key difference from sensorineural loss. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with longstanding bilateral tinnitus asks what medication will cure it. What should he be told?","choices":["Corticosteroids are curative","Diuretics eliminate it","Antihistamines are first-line","No drug has proved better than placebo","A short antibiotic course will clear it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — masking and biofeedback may help instead. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear 2026 - Dr. Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"}]};
